## ⚡ Performance Principles

### Premature Optimization is Evil
- **Measure first, optimize second**
- **Profile before you optimize**
- **Optimize the bottlenecks, not everything**
- **Readable code first, fast code second**

### Efficient Algorithms
- **Choose appropriate data structures**
- **Consider time/space complexity**
- **Avoid unnecessary loops and iterations**
- **Cache expensive computations**

```typescript
// ✅ GOOD: Efficient with caching
class UserService {
  private userCache = new Map<string, User>()
  
  async getUser(id: string): Promise<User> {
    // Check cache first
    if (this.userCache.has(id)) {
      return this.userCache.get(id)!
    }
    
    const user = await database.users.findById(id)
    this.userCache.set(id, user) // Cache for future use
    return user
  }
  
  // Batch operations for efficiency
  async getUsers(ids: string[]): Promise<User[]> {
    const uncachedIds = ids.filter(id => !this.userCache.has(id))
    
    if (uncachedIds.length > 0) {
      const users = await database.users.findByIds(uncachedIds)
      users.forEach(user => this.userCache.set(user.id, user))
    }
    
    return ids.map(id => this.userCache.get(id)!)
  }
}

// ❌ BAD: Inefficient, no caching
class UserService {
  async getUser(id: string): Promise<User> {
    return database.users.findById(id) // Always hits database
  }
  
  async getUsers(ids: string[]): Promise<User[]> {
    const users = []
    for (const id of ids) {
      users.push(await database.users.findById(id)) // N+1 query problem
    }
    return users
  }
}
```

## 🔒 Security Best Practices

### Input Validation
- **Validate all inputs** at boundaries
- **Sanitize user data** before processing
- **Use whitelist approach** (allow known good, reject everything else)
- **Never trust external data**

```typescript
// ✅ GOOD: Comprehensive input validation
function createBlogPost(input: unknown): BlogPost {
  const schema = z.object({
    title: z.string().min(1).max(200).trim(),
    content: z.string().min(10).max(10000),
    tags: z.array(z.string().regex(/^[a-zA-Z0-9-]+$/)).max(10),
    publishAt: z.date().min(new Date())
  })
  
  const validatedInput = schema.parse(input)
  
  // Sanitize HTML content
  const sanitizedContent = sanitizeHtml(validatedInput.content, {
    allowedTags: ['p', 'br', 'strong', 'em'],
    allowedAttributes: {}
  })
  
  return {
    ...validatedInput,
    content: sanitizedContent,
    slug: generateSlug(validatedInput.title)
  }
}

// ❌ BAD: No validation, security risks
function createBlogPost(input: any): BlogPost {
  return {
    title: input.title,
    content: input.content, // Could contain XSS
    tags: input.tags,
    publishAt: input.publishAt
  }
}
```

### Secure Coding Patterns
- **Principle of least privilege**
- **Defense in depth**
- **Fail securely** (deny by default)
- **Log security events**

## 📊 Testing Principles

### Test-Driven Development (TDD)
- **Red → Green → Refactor** cycle
- **Write tests first** when possible
- **Test behavior, not implementation**
- **One assertion per test**

```typescript
// ✅ GOOD: Clear, focused tests
describe('UserService', () => {
  describe('createUser', () => {
    it('should create user with valid data', async () => {
      const userData = createValidUserData()
      
      const result = await userService.createUser(userData)
      
      expect(result.id).toBeDefined()
      expect(result.email).toBe(userData.email)
      expect(result.password).not.toBe(userData.password) // Should be hashed
    })
    
    it('should throw error when email is invalid', async () => {
      const userData = createValidUserData({ email: 'invalid-email' })
      
      await expect(userService.createUser(userData))
        .rejects
        .toThrow('Please enter a valid email address')
    })
    
    it('should throw error when email already exists', async () => {
      const userData = createValidUserData()
      await userService.createUser(userData) // Create first user
      
      await expect(userService.createUser(userData))
        .rejects
        .toThrow('Email address is already registered')
    })
  })
})

// ❌ BAD: Unclear, multiple assertions
describe('UserService', () => {
  it('should work correctly', async () => {
    const userData = { email: 'test@example.com', password: 'password123' }
    const result = await userService.createUser(userData)
    expect(result.id).toBeDefined()
    expect(result.email).toBe(userData.email)
    
    // Testing different behavior in same test
    const invalidData = { email: 'invalid', password: 'short' }
    await expect(userService.createUser(invalidData)).rejects.toThrow()
  }
  )}
```

### Test Organization
- **Arrange, Act, Assert** pattern
- **Descriptive test names** that explain expected behavior
- **Independent tests** (no shared state)
- **Mock external dependencies**

## 🚫 Anti-Patterns to Avoid

### Code Smells
- **Long functions** (>20 lines)
- **Large classes** (>200 lines)
- **Deep nesting** (>3 levels)
- **Magic numbers** without constants
- **Duplicate code**
- **God objects** (classes that do everything)

### Bad Practices
- **Commenting out code** instead of deleting
- **TODO comments** that never get addressed
- **Hardcoded values** in business logic
- **Silent failures** (catching exceptions without handling)
- **Mixing abstraction levels**

```typescript
// ❌ AVOID: Multiple anti-patterns
class UserManager { // God object
  createUser(email, password, firstName, lastName, age, phone, address) { // Too many parameters
    if (email && password) { // Magic conditions
      if (password.length > 8) { // Magic number
        if (age > 18) { // Deep nesting
          // TODO: Add validation later
          const user = {
            email: email,
            password: this.hashPassword(password), // Mixed abstraction
            profile: {
              name: firstName + ' ' + lastName, // String concatenation
              age: age,
              phone: phone,
              address: address
            },
            created: new Date().toISOString(), // Different date format
            active: true
          }
          
          try {
            this.saveToDatabase(user)
            this.sendEmail(user.email) // Side effect
            return user
          } catch (e) {
            // Silent failure
            return null
          }
        }
      }
    }
    return null // What went wrong?
  }
}
```

## 📏 Code Review Standards

### Review Checklist
- **Functionality**: Does the code do what it's supposed to do?
- **Readability**: Can I understand this code in 6 months?
- **Performance**: Are there obvious performance issues?
- **Security**: Are there security vulnerabilities?
- **Tests**: Is the code properly tested?
- **Documentation**: Is complex logic documented?

### Review Culture
- **Be constructive**, not destructive
- **Explain the "why"** behind suggestions
- **Ask questions** instead of making demands
- **Praise good code** when you see it
- **Learn from others** during reviews

## 🎯 Success Metrics

### Code Quality Indicators
- **Cyclomatic complexity** < 10 per function
- **Test coverage** > 80%
- **Code duplication** < 3%
- **Average function length** < 15 lines
- **Documentation coverage** for public APIs

### Developer Experience Metrics
- **Time to understand new code** < 30 minutes
- **Time to fix bugs** decreasing over time
- **Code review turnaround** < 24 hours
- **Build/test time** < 5 minutes

